1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,742
I tested retrieval on my own notes.

2
00:00:02,742 --> 00:00:04,466
Forty-five thousand words,

3
00:00:04,466 --> 00:00:06,823
twenty-four questions I wrote myself,

4
00:00:06,823 --> 00:00:08,490
and three ways to search.

5
00:00:08,490 --> 00:00:10,215
With the right chunk size,

6
00:00:10,215 --> 00:00:14,182
the answer sat in the top three results ninety-two percent of the

7
00:00:14,182 --> 00:00:15,849
time. With the wrong one,

8
00:00:15,849 --> 00:00:16,826
seventy-nine.

9
00:00:17,176 --> 00:00:21,450
And the fancy fusion method that every guide recommends did not win

10
00:00:21,450 --> 00:00:22,353
in my test.

11
00:00:22,353 --> 00:00:24,821
This lesson explains how R A G works,

12
00:00:24,821 --> 00:00:27,530
and shows what a real test actually says,

13
00:00:27,530 --> 00:00:30,419
including the parts that did not go to plan.

14
00:00:31,669 --> 00:00:35,119
Welcome to lesson eight of the cloud for A I engineers course,

15
00:00:35,119 --> 00:00:36,426
on The Code Notebook.

16
00:00:36,776 --> 00:00:39,910
The handwritten notes and the code for this lesson are linked in

17
00:00:39,910 --> 00:00:40,832
the description.

18
00:00:42,082 --> 00:00:43,963
We now have the cloud pieces.

19
00:00:43,963 --> 00:00:45,216
Chips, containers,

20
00:00:45,216 --> 00:00:46,299
data, training,

21
00:00:46,299 --> 00:00:47,724
serving and shipping.

22
00:00:47,724 --> 00:00:50,402
From here we build the applications on top,

23
00:00:50,402 --> 00:00:52,567
starting with the most common one,

24
00:00:52,567 --> 00:00:54,562
retrieval augmented generation,

25
00:00:54,562 --> 00:00:55,530
called R A G.

26
00:00:55,880 --> 00:00:58,158
A note on honesty before we start.

27
00:00:58,158 --> 00:01:01,335
The search test uses the text of my own notebook,

28
00:01:01,335 --> 00:01:03,373
and questions I wrote by hand.

29
00:01:03,373 --> 00:01:05,531
The vector side uses a stand in,

30
00:01:05,531 --> 00:01:09,428
because no embedding model could be downloaded on my machine.

31
00:01:09,428 --> 00:01:11,286
I will label it every time.

32
00:01:12,536 --> 00:01:13,988
Why does R A G exist?

33
00:01:13,988 --> 00:01:17,588
A language model only knows what was in its training data.

34
00:01:17,588 --> 00:01:20,259
It does not know your company's documents,

35
00:01:20,259 --> 00:01:21,362
today's prices,

36
00:01:21,362 --> 00:01:22,872
or last week's policy.

37
00:01:22,872 --> 00:01:24,382
And if you ask anyway,

38
00:01:24,382 --> 00:01:26,704
it may confidently invent an answer.

39
00:01:27,054 --> 00:01:29,600
R A G fixes this like an open book exam.

40
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:31,220
When a question arrives,

41
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:33,593
you first look up the relevant pages,

42
00:01:33,593 --> 00:01:35,386
and put them in the prompt.

43
00:01:35,386 --> 00:01:37,932
The model then answers from those pages,

44
00:01:37,932 --> 00:01:39,379
ideally quoting them.

45
00:01:40,629 --> 00:01:43,334
A real R A G pipeline has seven steps.

46
00:01:43,334 --> 00:01:44,623
Parse the files.

47
00:01:44,623 --> 00:01:46,362
Chunk them into pieces.

48
00:01:46,362 --> 00:01:48,295
Add context to each piece.

49
00:01:48,295 --> 00:01:50,357
Embed the pieces as vectors.

50
00:01:50,707 --> 00:01:52,194
Then, at question time,

51
00:01:52,194 --> 00:01:54,507
search with both keywords and vectors.

52
00:01:54,507 --> 00:01:57,040
Rerank the results so the best come first.

53
00:01:57,390 --> 00:02:00,455
And finally generate an answer with citations.

54
00:02:00,455 --> 00:02:03,826
The mnemonic is pretty cats can eat hot red grapes,

55
00:02:03,826 --> 00:02:04,684
for parse,

56
00:02:04,684 --> 00:02:05,849
chunk, context,

57
00:02:05,849 --> 00:02:06,952
embed, hybrid,

58
00:02:06,952 --> 00:02:08,239
rerank, generate.

59
00:02:09,489 --> 00:02:10,257
Step one,

60
00:02:10,257 --> 00:02:12,502
parse. Turn files into clean text.

61
00:02:12,502 --> 00:02:15,396
This is where many projects quietly go wrong.

62
00:02:15,396 --> 00:02:18,939
A table flattened into a line of words can turn a column

63
00:02:18,939 --> 00:02:20,593
of prices into nonsense,

64
00:02:20,593 --> 00:02:24,787
and your answers will quote the wrong number with total confidence.

65
00:02:25,137 --> 00:02:27,357
Use a parser that understands layout,

66
00:02:27,357 --> 00:02:29,470
and keep metadata with every chunk.

67
00:02:29,470 --> 00:02:30,282
The source,

68
00:02:30,282 --> 00:02:30,986
the date,

69
00:02:30,986 --> 00:02:32,882
the tenant and who may read it.

70
00:02:32,882 --> 00:02:35,590
You will need that metadata for filters later.

71
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:37,578
Step two,

72
00:02:37,578 --> 00:02:40,643
chunk. A whole document is too big to search well,

73
00:02:40,643 --> 00:02:42,118
so cut it into pieces.

74
00:02:42,118 --> 00:02:45,693
The rule of thumb is three hundred to eight hundred tokens,

75
00:02:45,693 --> 00:02:48,815
with some overlap so a sentence is not cut in half.

76
00:02:49,165 --> 00:02:50,073
Common ways:

77
00:02:50,073 --> 00:02:51,095
fixed windows,

78
00:02:51,095 --> 00:02:53,479
splitting at paragraphs and sentences,

79
00:02:53,479 --> 00:02:55,636
splitting where the topic changes,

80
00:02:55,636 --> 00:02:59,496
or keeping small child chunks but returning their larger parent.

81
00:02:59,496 --> 00:03:01,312
I tested window size myself,

82
00:03:01,312 --> 00:03:03,526
so let us see what the numbers say.

83
00:03:04,776 --> 00:03:05,591
Step four,

84
00:03:05,591 --> 00:03:09,144
embed. An embedding model turns each chunk into a vector,

85
00:03:09,144 --> 00:03:11,649
so similar meanings sit close together.

86
00:03:11,649 --> 00:03:13,396
You embed the chunks once,

87
00:03:13,396 --> 00:03:17,648
offline. At question time you embed the question and find the nearest

88
00:03:17,648 --> 00:03:18,289
chunks.

89
00:03:18,639 --> 00:03:21,306
Cache embeddings by the hash of the text,

90
00:03:21,306 --> 00:03:24,210
so unchanged chunks are never paid for twice.

91
00:03:24,210 --> 00:03:27,410
And always record which embedding model made them.

92
00:03:27,410 --> 00:03:30,729
Mixing two models in one index quietly ruins search.

93
00:03:31,979 --> 00:03:34,151
A tip that connects to lesson four.

94
00:03:34,151 --> 00:03:35,488
Vectors cost memory,

95
00:03:35,488 --> 00:03:36,546
so shrink them.

96
00:03:36,546 --> 00:03:40,333
Some embedding models are trained so that you can simply cut off

97
00:03:40,333 --> 00:03:42,784
the end of each vector and keep working,

98
00:03:42,784 --> 00:03:44,956
losing a little quality gracefully.

99
00:03:44,956 --> 00:03:47,129
They are called Matryoshka vectors,

100
00:03:47,129 --> 00:03:49,078
after the nested Russian dolls.

101
00:03:49,428 --> 00:03:52,392
Combine that with half size or one byte numbers,

102
00:03:52,392 --> 00:03:55,697
and a large index can fit in a fraction of the memory.

103
00:03:55,697 --> 00:03:59,744
Always check recall on your own questions after shrinking anything.

104
00:04:00,994 --> 00:04:01,780
Step five,

105
00:04:01,780 --> 00:04:02,792
hybrid search.

106
00:04:02,792 --> 00:04:04,589
Vectors are good at meaning.

107
00:04:04,589 --> 00:04:08,409
A question phrased differently from the document still finds it.

108
00:04:08,409 --> 00:04:10,712
But vectors are weak at exact things,

109
00:04:10,712 --> 00:04:12,005
like an error code,

110
00:04:12,005 --> 00:04:13,746
a product number or a name.

111
00:04:14,096 --> 00:04:15,199
Keyword search,

112
00:04:15,199 --> 00:04:16,766
called B M twenty-five,

113
00:04:16,766 --> 00:04:17,927
is the opposite.

114
00:04:17,927 --> 00:04:19,088
Exact terms win,

115
00:04:19,088 --> 00:04:20,307
paraphrases lose.

116
00:04:20,307 --> 00:04:21,236
So run both,

117
00:04:21,236 --> 00:04:22,861
and merge the two lists.

118
00:04:24,111 --> 00:04:27,939
How do you merge two lists whose scores mean different things?

119
00:04:27,939 --> 00:04:30,781
The popular answer is reciprocal rank fusion.

120
00:04:30,781 --> 00:04:33,391
Ignore the scores and use only the ranks.

121
00:04:33,391 --> 00:04:36,870
Each list gives a document one over sixty plus its rank.

122
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:40,777
A document that is first in the vector list and third in

123
00:04:40,777 --> 00:04:45,105
the keyword list scores one over sixty-one plus one over sixty-three,

124
00:04:45,105 --> 00:04:47,180
about point oh three two three.

125
00:04:47,180 --> 00:04:50,915
A document found by only one list scores about point oh one

126
00:04:50,915 --> 00:04:52,575
six. Found by both wins.

127
00:04:53,825 --> 00:04:55,839
A small trick before searching.

128
00:04:55,839 --> 00:04:57,106
Users write short,

129
00:04:57,106 --> 00:04:58,257
vague questions,

130
00:04:58,257 --> 00:04:59,408
so rewrite them.

131
00:04:59,408 --> 00:05:03,610
One method asks the model to write a hypothetical answer and searches

132
00:05:03,610 --> 00:05:04,416
with that,

133
00:05:04,416 --> 00:05:07,928
because answer shaped text is closer to answer documents.

134
00:05:08,278 --> 00:05:12,057
Another splits a complex question into several smaller ones,

135
00:05:12,057 --> 00:05:14,064
searches for each in parallel,

136
00:05:14,064 --> 00:05:14,950
and merges.

137
00:05:14,950 --> 00:05:16,957
It costs one extra model call,

138
00:05:16,957 --> 00:05:18,847
so it adds a little latency.

139
00:05:18,847 --> 00:05:21,858
Use it when questions are genuinely multi part.

140
00:05:23,108 --> 00:05:24,323
Documents change,

141
00:05:24,323 --> 00:05:26,753
and a stale index gives stale answers.

142
00:05:26,753 --> 00:05:28,952
Connect the index to your storage,

143
00:05:28,952 --> 00:05:31,903
so a changed file is parsed and embedded again,

144
00:05:31,903 --> 00:05:34,449
and a deleted file's chunks are removed.

145
00:05:34,449 --> 00:05:36,012
Hash each chunk's text,

146
00:05:36,012 --> 00:05:38,963
so you only pay to embed what actually changed.

147
00:05:39,313 --> 00:05:41,580
Also store the date with each chunk,

148
00:05:41,580 --> 00:05:44,867
and decide what should win when two versions disagree.

149
00:05:44,867 --> 00:05:48,665
A search that returns last year's policy next to this year's is

150
00:05:48,665 --> 00:05:50,082
worse than no search.

151
00:05:51,332 --> 00:05:52,095
Step six,

152
00:05:52,095 --> 00:05:54,385
rerank. Retrieve a generous number,

153
00:05:54,385 --> 00:05:56,030
say a hundred and fifty,

154
00:05:56,030 --> 00:05:57,321
then use a slower,

155
00:05:57,321 --> 00:06:01,432
smarter model that reads the question and each candidate together,

156
00:06:01,432 --> 00:06:03,135
and keep the best twenty.

157
00:06:03,135 --> 00:06:05,895
Managed rerankers exist on every big cloud.

158
00:06:06,245 --> 00:06:07,653
Tune the final number.

159
00:06:07,653 --> 00:06:10,630
Twenty beat ten and five in the notebook's reading.

160
00:06:10,630 --> 00:06:13,067
But stuffing too many passages backfires,

161
00:06:13,067 --> 00:06:16,586
because models notice the start and end of a long prompt more

162
00:06:16,586 --> 00:06:17,669
than the middle.

163
00:06:18,919 --> 00:06:19,804
Step seven,

164
00:06:19,804 --> 00:06:21,987
generate. Give each chunk an I D.

165
00:06:21,987 --> 00:06:24,996
Tell the model to answer only from the sources,

166
00:06:24,996 --> 00:06:26,235
to cite the I Ds,

167
00:06:26,235 --> 00:06:29,126
and to say not found if the answer is absent.

168
00:06:29,126 --> 00:06:31,132
That last instruction matters.

169
00:06:31,132 --> 00:06:32,017
Without it,

170
00:06:32,017 --> 00:06:34,260
the model fills gaps with guesses.

171
00:06:34,610 --> 00:06:39,151
Some model providers offer a citations feature that returns the exact quoted

172
00:06:39,151 --> 00:06:40,399
spans. Either way,

173
00:06:40,399 --> 00:06:43,975
users should be able to click from an answer to its source.

174
00:06:45,225 --> 00:06:46,169
Now my test.

175
00:06:46,169 --> 00:06:49,296
The corpus is the text of this course's notebook,

176
00:06:49,296 --> 00:06:51,420
about forty-five thousand words.

177
00:06:51,420 --> 00:06:53,425
I wrote twenty-four questions,

178
00:06:53,425 --> 00:06:54,133
such as,

179
00:06:54,133 --> 00:06:57,732
how long does S three take to auto partition after a load

180
00:06:57,732 --> 00:06:59,148
spike, and for each,

181
00:06:59,148 --> 00:07:01,330
the exact phrase that answers it.

182
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:04,849
A chunk counts as a hit if it contains that phrase.

183
00:07:04,849 --> 00:07:08,480
Hit at three means the answer is somewhere in the top three

184
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:10,669
chunks. I compared keyword search,

185
00:07:10,669 --> 00:07:13,781
a stand in for vectors built from word statistics,

186
00:07:13,781 --> 00:07:15,336
and the fusion of both.

187
00:07:15,336 --> 00:07:18,160
The stand in is not a neural embedding model.

188
00:07:19,410 --> 00:07:20,553
First, chunk size.

189
00:07:20,553 --> 00:07:22,059
With hundred word chunks,

190
00:07:22,059 --> 00:07:26,008
keyword search found the answer in the top three eighty-three percent of

191
00:07:26,008 --> 00:07:26,683
the time.

192
00:07:26,683 --> 00:07:28,190
With three hundred words,

193
00:07:28,190 --> 00:07:29,644
eighty-seven and a half.

194
00:07:29,644 --> 00:07:30,839
With eight hundred,

195
00:07:30,839 --> 00:07:31,723
seventy-nine.

196
00:07:32,073 --> 00:07:34,674
The vector stand in followed the same shape.

197
00:07:34,674 --> 00:07:36,246
Eighty-three, ninety-two,

198
00:07:36,246 --> 00:07:39,280
eighty-eight. Three hundred words was best for both.

199
00:07:40,530 --> 00:07:42,015
Why does the middle win?

200
00:07:42,015 --> 00:07:44,879
Chunks that are too small lose their surroundings.

201
00:07:44,879 --> 00:07:48,645
A line like k equals sixty means little without the sentence around

202
00:07:48,645 --> 00:07:49,016
it.

203
00:07:49,366 --> 00:07:51,880
Chunks that are too big dilute the match,

204
00:07:51,880 --> 00:07:55,678
because one long chunk holds many topics and ranks well for none

205
00:07:55,678 --> 00:07:56,349
of them.

206
00:07:56,349 --> 00:07:59,644
That agrees with the three to eight hundred token rule.

207
00:07:59,644 --> 00:08:02,437
The exact best size depends on your documents,

208
00:08:02,437 --> 00:08:03,275
so test it.

209
00:08:04,525 --> 00:08:05,673
Now the surprise.

210
00:08:05,673 --> 00:08:07,588
With three hundred word chunks,

211
00:08:07,588 --> 00:08:10,596
keyword search got eighty-seven and a half percent.

212
00:08:10,596 --> 00:08:11,908
The vector stand in,

213
00:08:11,908 --> 00:08:13,385
ninety-one point seven.

214
00:08:13,385 --> 00:08:14,861
And the fusion of both,

215
00:08:14,861 --> 00:08:16,393
eighty-seven and a half.

216
00:08:16,743 --> 00:08:18,551
I expected the fusion to win.

217
00:08:18,551 --> 00:08:19,373
It did not.

218
00:08:19,723 --> 00:08:22,958
It matched keyword search and trailed the vector stand in.

219
00:08:22,958 --> 00:08:23,897
Honest result,

220
00:08:23,897 --> 00:08:25,045
from a small test.

221
00:08:26,295 --> 00:08:28,322
Why? I counted who found what.

222
00:08:28,322 --> 00:08:32,554
Twenty-one of the twenty-four questions were found by both methods.

223
00:08:32,554 --> 00:08:34,879
Nothing was found only by keywords.

224
00:08:34,879 --> 00:08:37,145
One was found only by the vectors.

225
00:08:37,145 --> 00:08:39,052
And two were missed by both.

226
00:08:39,402 --> 00:08:43,094
Fusion helps when the two methods fail on different questions.

227
00:08:43,094 --> 00:08:44,045
On my corpus,

228
00:08:44,045 --> 00:08:45,444
the two mostly agree,

229
00:08:45,444 --> 00:08:47,737
so there is little for fusion to add.

230
00:08:47,737 --> 00:08:49,416
Your documents may differ.

231
00:08:49,416 --> 00:08:51,430
Codes and names favour keywords.

232
00:08:52,680 --> 00:08:55,784
The two questions both missed are instructive.

233
00:08:55,784 --> 00:08:59,260
How much notice does A W S give for a spot instance.

234
00:08:59,260 --> 00:09:02,737
And what value of k does reciprocal rank fusion use.

235
00:09:03,087 --> 00:09:05,282
The first is about spot instances,

236
00:09:05,282 --> 00:09:08,691
a word that appears in many chunks across the notebook,

237
00:09:08,691 --> 00:09:10,539
so dozens of chunks compete.

238
00:09:10,539 --> 00:09:13,139
The second has an answer made of symbols,

239
00:09:13,139 --> 00:09:14,236
k equals sixty,

240
00:09:14,236 --> 00:09:16,720
which my keyword tokenizer throws away.

241
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:19,955
Reading failures like this is called error analysis,

242
00:09:19,955 --> 00:09:22,151
and it tells you what to fix next.

243
00:09:23,401 --> 00:09:25,246
A caution about my own test.

244
00:09:25,246 --> 00:09:27,263
Twenty-four questions is small.

245
00:09:27,263 --> 00:09:30,723
Each question is worth four point two percentage points,

246
00:09:30,723 --> 00:09:33,317
so differences of one question are noise.

247
00:09:33,317 --> 00:09:34,701
I trust the pattern,

248
00:09:34,701 --> 00:09:37,353
three hundred words beating eight hundred,

249
00:09:37,353 --> 00:09:39,025
more than any single gap.

250
00:09:39,375 --> 00:09:41,607
The point is not that fusion is bad.

251
00:09:41,607 --> 00:09:45,347
The notebook cites larger benchmarks where hybrid search helps.

252
00:09:45,347 --> 00:09:48,137
The point is to build an eval set of your own,

253
00:09:48,137 --> 00:09:51,932
because it takes an afternoon and tells you what really works on

254
00:09:51,932 --> 00:09:52,992
your documents.

255
00:09:54,242 --> 00:09:56,983
Next, an idea called contextual retrieval.

256
00:09:56,983 --> 00:09:58,174
Before indexing,

257
00:09:58,174 --> 00:10:01,987
ask a model to write one or two sentences placing each chunk

258
00:10:01,987 --> 00:10:03,178
in its document,

259
00:10:03,178 --> 00:10:04,429
and prepend them.

260
00:10:04,429 --> 00:10:06,931
The original study reported that this,

261
00:10:06,931 --> 00:10:08,063
with reranking,

262
00:10:08,063 --> 00:10:10,982
cut failed retrievals by sixty-seven percent.

263
00:10:11,332 --> 00:10:13,764
I tried the cheapest possible version.

264
00:10:13,764 --> 00:10:17,469
I just prepended the topic title to each hundred word chunk.

265
00:10:17,469 --> 00:10:19,437
Keyword search did not change.

266
00:10:19,437 --> 00:10:23,721
The vector stand in went from eighty-three to eighty-seven and a half,

267
00:10:23,721 --> 00:10:24,705
one question.

268
00:10:24,705 --> 00:10:26,616
A title is very weak context.

269
00:10:27,866 --> 00:10:29,918
I could not run the real method,

270
00:10:29,918 --> 00:10:33,451
which uses a language model to write each chunk's context.

271
00:10:33,451 --> 00:10:35,958
So I cannot confirm sixty-seven percent.

272
00:10:35,958 --> 00:10:38,580
That figure comes from the original study,

273
00:10:38,580 --> 00:10:40,290
described in the notebook.

274
00:10:40,290 --> 00:10:43,424
What I can say is that a plain title is not enough.

275
00:10:43,774 --> 00:10:45,649
The real method is affordable,

276
00:10:45,649 --> 00:10:48,352
about one dollar per million document tokens,

277
00:10:48,352 --> 00:10:50,062
once, using prompt caching.

278
00:10:50,062 --> 00:10:51,993
If your search quality matters,

279
00:10:51,993 --> 00:10:54,530
it is among the first things worth trying.

280
00:10:55,780 --> 00:10:58,897
Researchers listed seven ways R A G systems fail.

281
00:10:58,897 --> 00:11:00,074
Missing content,

282
00:11:00,074 --> 00:11:02,250
so the answer is not in the data.

283
00:11:02,250 --> 00:11:05,132
The right document is not ranked high enough.

284
00:11:05,132 --> 00:11:07,896
It was found but dropped before the prompt.

285
00:11:08,246 --> 00:11:10,999
It is in the prompt but the model ignores it.

286
00:11:10,999 --> 00:11:12,348
The format is wrong.

287
00:11:12,348 --> 00:11:13,922
The answer is too vague.

288
00:11:13,922 --> 00:11:15,271
Or it is incomplete.

289
00:11:15,271 --> 00:11:17,350
Each failure has a different fix,

290
00:11:17,350 --> 00:11:20,385
and only an eval set tells you which one you have.

291
00:11:21,635 --> 00:11:23,091
Do you even need R A G?

292
00:11:23,091 --> 00:11:27,189
If your whole knowledge base is under about two hundred thousand tokens,

293
00:11:27,189 --> 00:11:28,860
roughly five hundred pages,

294
00:11:28,860 --> 00:11:30,262
and it rarely changes,

295
00:11:30,262 --> 00:11:31,448
and has one owner,

296
00:11:31,448 --> 00:11:34,306
you can put all of it in the prompt and cache it.

297
00:11:34,656 --> 00:11:38,265
Caching can give more than twice the speed and up to ninety

298
00:11:38,265 --> 00:11:39,582
percent lower cost,

299
00:11:39,582 --> 00:11:41,014
according to vendors.

300
00:11:41,014 --> 00:11:42,159
If it is larger,

301
00:11:42,159 --> 00:11:43,706
changing, permissioned,

302
00:11:43,706 --> 00:11:45,424
or shared by many tenants,

303
00:11:45,424 --> 00:11:46,226
use R A G.

304
00:11:46,226 --> 00:11:48,574
Cost per question grows with context,

305
00:11:48,574 --> 00:11:50,923
and models miss things in the middle.

306
00:11:52,173 --> 00:11:54,463
Permissions deserve their own warning.

307
00:11:54,463 --> 00:11:56,861
If your documents have different owners,

308
00:11:56,861 --> 00:11:59,588
every chunk carries a list of who may read it,

309
00:11:59,588 --> 00:12:02,314
and the search must filter by the asking user.

310
00:12:02,314 --> 00:12:05,857
Retrieving first and filtering afterwards is not good enough.

311
00:12:06,207 --> 00:12:11,302
A missing filter means one customer's data appears in another customer's answer.

312
00:12:11,302 --> 00:12:14,213
That is the most serious R A G bug there is,

313
00:12:14,213 --> 00:12:16,215
and it is caused by metadata,

314
00:12:16,215 --> 00:12:17,488
not by the model.

315
00:12:18,738 --> 00:12:20,005
One more warning,

316
00:12:20,005 --> 00:12:22,177
which lesson ten covers in full.

317
00:12:22,177 --> 00:12:25,012
Text you retrieve is not your instructions.

318
00:12:25,012 --> 00:12:28,933
A document can contain sentences written to hijack the model.

319
00:12:28,933 --> 00:12:30,924
Treat retrieved text as data,

320
00:12:30,924 --> 00:12:32,251
never as commands.

321
00:12:32,601 --> 00:12:33,219
For now,

322
00:12:33,219 --> 00:12:36,976
remember to keep the instruction part of the prompt separate from the

323
00:12:36,976 --> 00:12:37,954
retrieved part,

324
00:12:37,954 --> 00:12:41,042
and never let retrieved text grant the model new powers.

325
00:12:42,292 --> 00:12:44,189
How do you know a change helped?

326
00:12:44,189 --> 00:12:46,139
Keep a labelled set of questions,

327
00:12:46,139 --> 00:12:48,563
with the source passage that answers each.

328
00:12:48,563 --> 00:12:51,250
Measure whether the right passage is retrieved,

329
00:12:51,250 --> 00:12:52,462
called recall at k,

330
00:12:52,462 --> 00:12:55,308
separately from whether the final answer is right.

331
00:12:55,658 --> 00:12:57,399
Track both on every release.

332
00:12:57,399 --> 00:13:00,881
Retrieval failures and answer failures need different fixes,

333
00:13:00,881 --> 00:13:03,711
and mixing them makes debugging a guessing game.

334
00:13:04,961 --> 00:13:07,304
Two advanced ideas to know by name.

335
00:13:07,304 --> 00:13:11,030
Graph R A G builds a graph of entities from your documents

336
00:13:11,030 --> 00:13:13,613
and answers big picture questions like,

337
00:13:13,613 --> 00:13:15,356
what are the main themes.

338
00:13:15,356 --> 00:13:17,399
It beats plain R A G on those.

339
00:13:17,399 --> 00:13:18,781
For pinpoint facts,

340
00:13:18,781 --> 00:13:21,365
hybrid search with reranking is enough.

341
00:13:21,715 --> 00:13:24,585
Agentic R A G lets the model plan searches,

342
00:13:24,585 --> 00:13:26,356
pick sources and iterate.

343
00:13:26,356 --> 00:13:28,554
Better for multi step questions,

344
00:13:28,554 --> 00:13:30,569
but more calls and more cost.

345
00:13:30,569 --> 00:13:31,791
Both are extras.

346
00:13:31,791 --> 00:13:33,867
Get the basics measured first.

347
00:13:35,117 --> 00:13:37,662
Every big cloud sells managed R A G.

348
00:13:37,662 --> 00:13:39,888
Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases,

349
00:13:39,888 --> 00:13:41,542
Google's R A G Engine,

350
00:13:41,542 --> 00:13:43,960
Azure A I Search with Foundry I Q,

351
00:13:43,960 --> 00:13:45,805
and OpenAI's file search.

352
00:13:45,805 --> 00:13:47,459
They handle ingestion,

353
00:13:47,459 --> 00:13:48,986
chunking, embedding,

354
00:13:48,986 --> 00:13:51,785
storage, retrieval and grounded answers,

355
00:13:51,785 --> 00:13:54,266
and all offer a retrieve only call.

356
00:13:54,616 --> 00:13:58,147
Pick by where your data and your identity already live.

357
00:13:58,147 --> 00:14:00,541
SharePoint and Entra point to Azure.

358
00:14:00,541 --> 00:14:02,337
S three points to Bedrock.

359
00:14:02,337 --> 00:14:05,210
Google storage and BigQuery point to Google.

360
00:14:05,210 --> 00:14:07,604
Minimal operations points to OpenAI,

361
00:14:07,604 --> 00:14:09,938
though your data leaves your cloud.

362
00:14:11,188 --> 00:14:12,605
For ShopNest Copilot.

363
00:14:12,605 --> 00:14:14,930
Parse the help articles and policies,

364
00:14:14,930 --> 00:14:17,878
chunk at about three hundred words with overlap,

365
00:14:17,878 --> 00:14:20,939
store the source and access level with each chunk,

366
00:14:20,939 --> 00:14:23,717
and embed once with a recorded model version.

367
00:14:24,067 --> 00:14:26,258
Search with keywords and vectors,

368
00:14:26,258 --> 00:14:29,514
rerank, answer with citations and a not found rule,

369
00:14:29,514 --> 00:14:31,112
and filter by customer.

370
00:14:31,112 --> 00:14:34,605
Then build a forty question eval set from real tickets,

371
00:14:34,605 --> 00:14:37,802
and measure recall before touching anything fancy.

372
00:14:39,052 --> 00:14:40,870
Four words to carry forward.

373
00:14:40,870 --> 00:14:42,745
Chunk: a piece of a document,

374
00:14:42,745 --> 00:14:43,938
sized for search.

375
00:14:43,938 --> 00:14:44,960
Hybrid search:

376
00:14:44,960 --> 00:14:46,892
keywords and vectors together.

377
00:14:47,242 --> 00:14:50,438
Rerank: sorting the shortlist with a smarter model.

378
00:14:50,438 --> 00:14:51,426
And hit at k:

379
00:14:51,426 --> 00:14:55,435
the share of questions whose answer appears in the top k results.

380
00:14:56,685 --> 00:14:57,804
Interview corner.

381
00:14:57,804 --> 00:14:59,724
Asked how you choose chunk size,

382
00:14:59,724 --> 00:15:03,615
say: start around three hundred to eight hundred tokens with overlap,

383
00:15:03,615 --> 00:15:05,694
then measure on your own questions.

384
00:15:05,694 --> 00:15:06,494
In my test,

385
00:15:06,494 --> 00:15:10,546
three hundred words found the answer in the top three ninety-two percent

386
00:15:10,546 --> 00:15:11,399
of the time,

387
00:15:11,399 --> 00:15:13,691
against seventy-nine for eight hundred.

388
00:15:14,041 --> 00:15:15,736
Asked why hybrid search,

389
00:15:15,736 --> 00:15:19,733
say: vectors catch paraphrases and keywords catch exact terms,

390
00:15:19,733 --> 00:15:21,973
merged by reciprocal rank fusion.

391
00:15:21,973 --> 00:15:23,668
But verify on your data.

392
00:15:23,668 --> 00:15:24,576
In my test,

393
00:15:24,576 --> 00:15:27,241
fusion did not beat the vector stand in,

394
00:15:27,241 --> 00:15:30,692
because the two methods failed on the same questions.

395
00:15:31,942 --> 00:15:32,736
Your turn.

396
00:15:32,736 --> 00:15:36,483
The zip for this lesson has two scripts and the notebook text.

397
00:15:36,483 --> 00:15:37,788
Run the evaluation.

398
00:15:37,788 --> 00:15:40,286
Then replace my questions with your own,

399
00:15:40,286 --> 00:15:41,876
from your own documents,

400
00:15:41,876 --> 00:15:44,260
and see which chunk size wins for you.

401
00:15:44,610 --> 00:15:47,246
Add a question whose answer is an error code,

402
00:15:47,246 --> 00:15:49,182
and watch which method finds it.

403
00:15:49,182 --> 00:15:52,517
One changed question teaches more than an hour of reading.

404
00:15:53,767 --> 00:15:57,005
Recap. R A G is an open book exam in seven steps,

405
00:15:57,005 --> 00:15:58,043
parse, chunk,

406
00:15:58,043 --> 00:15:59,204
context, embed,

407
00:15:59,204 --> 00:16:00,364
hybrid, rerank,

408
00:16:00,364 --> 00:16:01,891
generate. On my test,

409
00:16:01,891 --> 00:16:06,412
three hundred word chunks found the answer in the top three ninety-two

410
00:16:06,412 --> 00:16:07,878
percent of the time,

411
00:16:07,878 --> 00:16:09,222
and eight hundred,

412
00:16:09,222 --> 00:16:10,260
seventy-nine.

413
00:16:10,610 --> 00:16:11,978
Fusion did not win,

414
00:16:11,978 --> 00:16:15,190
because both methods failed on the same questions,

415
00:16:15,190 --> 00:16:17,391
and a title prefix barely helped.

416
00:16:17,391 --> 00:16:18,521
Filter by user,

417
00:16:18,521 --> 00:16:20,484
treat retrieved text as data,

418
00:16:20,484 --> 00:16:22,388
and build your own eval set.

419
00:16:22,388 --> 00:16:23,340
Next lesson:

420
00:16:23,340 --> 00:16:25,362
agents, tools and reliability.

421
00:16:26,612 --> 00:16:28,033
In the next lesson:

422
00:16:28,033 --> 00:16:30,134
agents, tools and reliability.

423
00:16:30,134 --> 00:16:31,432
Loops, protocols,

424
00:16:31,432 --> 00:16:33,038
retries and sandboxes.

425
00:16:33,388 --> 00:16:34,980
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

426
